🐛 Describe the bug
There's clang and there's MSVC, and they differ on what the expect in terms of capturing constant values (declared const).
Example code:
int foo() {
  constexpr const int kValue = 100;

  // The lambda below compileable by gcc/clang,  but fails for VC++,  since it expects
  // even constant expressions  to be captured.
  auto lam1 = []() { return kValue + 1; };

  // The lambda below compileable by all compilers, but trigers `unused-lambda-capture`
  // warning in clang, as constant expressions does not need to be captured, per
  // A lambda expression can read the value of a variable without capturing it
  //  -  if the variable is constexpr
  // see https://en.cppreference.com/w/cpp/language/lambda
  auto lam2 = [kValue]() { return kValue + 1; };

  return lam1() + lam2();
}
